<h2>Doors on the Model — Who May Use It, and How You May Call It</h2>

<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1 are <strong>one model with the same capabilities</strong>. Anyone can call Fable 5.1 through the API; Mythos 5.1 opens only to Project Glasswing participants (from 6 October also to security organizations verified through the Cyber Verification Program).</li>
    <li>The price dropped <strong>on a single line</strong> — cache reads, from $1 to $0.25 per million tokens. Base input and output rates are unchanged from Fable 5.</li>
    <li>The same release changed <strong>three things about how you call it</strong>. You cannot force a tool call, earlier models cannot read its reasoning, and editing the conversation history invalidates the reasoning that came after.</li>
    <li>Three weeks later <strong>the same rules arrived on Opus 5.5</strong> (22 September). What had been the terms of a specialty model became the terms of the model you get by default, and this time the price fell on the base rate rather than on cache reads.</li>
    <li>In October <strong>the rest of the 5.5 generation arrived</strong> (Sonnet 5.5 on 28 September, Haiku 5.5 on 7 October). The same calling rules reached the cheapest tier, and thinking blocks are now also tied to the account that made them. The door on dangerous capabilities split from one into <strong>three</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>One model, two names</h3>
<p>
    Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1, both out on September 1, have <strong>identical capabilities, specs and pricing.</strong> The only difference is who may call them. Fable 5.1 is open to every customer on the Claude API and through AWS, Google Cloud and Microsoft Foundry. Mythos 5.1 goes only to approved customers in <strong>Project Glasswing</strong>, Anthropic's program for securing software in fields where the stakes run high, such as cybersecurity and the life sciences. The same capability ships under two names, with a door on one of them.
</p>
<p>
    The specs are a 1M token context window and up to 128k output tokens, with the same per-token price across the whole window. Adaptive thinking is always on and cannot be turned off; <code>effort</code> controls its depth. Both models carry 30-day data retention, so neither is available under zero data retention without express authorization.
</p>
<p>
    One more thing splits them. <strong>Mythos 5.1 does not run the history-editing check</strong> described below. The same model can therefore be subject to different conditions, which is worth remembering if your code moves between the two names.
</p>

<h3>The price dropped on the cache line</h3>
<p>
    Only one cell in the table changed. Base input stays at $10 per million tokens, output at $50, five-minute cache writes at $12.50 and one-hour cache writes at $20, exactly as on Fable 5. <strong>Cache reads went from $1 to $0.25.</strong> As a ratio, what used to be 0.1 times the base input price is now <strong>0.025 times</strong>, against 0.1 on every other Claude model. The 512-token minimum cacheable length is unchanged.
</p>
<p>
    One cell moves the whole bill because of how agents work. A long session re-reads its opening every turn: the system prompt, the tool definitions, the conversation so far. When that opening sits in the cache, most of what gets billed is <strong>cache reads</strong>, so cutting that line to a quarter pulls the total down noticeably. The "25% on typical workloads, up to 45% on highly agentic work" figures from launch coverage all come out of this one line.
</p>
<p>
    The model itself is better than Fable 5 at agentic coding over long sessions, at documents, spreadsheets and slides, at multistep research, at reading charts and tables buried in dense PDFs, at reasoning across the full 1M context, and at operating a browser or desktop. The gap widens at higher effort. But as the price table shows, <strong>the improvement arrived as a discount on the cache rather than a markup on the model.</strong> Anthropic's own guidance is to start most workloads on <a href="#!news-20260724-claude-opus-5">Opus 5</a> and move to Fable 5.1 when Opus 5 at higher effort still falls short.
</p>

<h3>Three changes in how you call it</h3>
<p>
    Swap the model ID on the strength of the price alone and you snag in three places.
</p>
<p>
    First, <strong>you cannot force a tool call.</strong> Putting <code>any</code> or a specific tool name in <code>tool_choice</code> to pin the turn to that tool now returns a 400 error. The documentation gives the reason: thinking is always on for this model, and forcing a tool call skips it, so the model ends up <strong>writing its working-out into the tool arguments</strong> instead. Argument quality drops. Leave <code>tool_choice</code> at <code>auto</code> and set <code>strict: true</code> on the tool to enforce the schema, or say in the prompt when the tool applies.
</p>
<p>
    Second, <strong>thinking blocks</strong> — the fragments that carry the reasoning a model ran before answering and stay in the history — now have a direction. Fable 5.1 reads blocks from Opus 5, Fable 5 and earlier models, but none of those can read its own. When a router switches models or a refusal fallback drops to an older one, the API strips the blocks that model cannot read and lets the request through. The stripped tokens are not billed, but that model <strong>re-plans from nothing.</strong> Without the beta header, the strip happens silently.
</p>
<p>
    The third change touches the most code. A Fable 5.1 thinking block is valid only against the <strong>system prompt, tool list and conversation history that preceded it.</strong> Editing, reordering or removing an earlier turn, injecting a reminder line into an earlier turn and taking it out on the next request, rebuilding <code>system</code> or <code>tools</code> mid-conversation — each invalidates every thinking block after it. What does not invalidate them: removing a leading run of thinking blocks oldest first, server-side compaction and context editing, moving <code>cache_control</code> markers, and changing <code>effort</code> between requests.
</p>
<p>
    The check is enforced by default only for <strong>API accounts created on or after August 31, 2026.</strong> Older accounts record the mismatch and move on. And if a tool manages the history for you — Claude Code, claude.ai, Managed Agents, the Agent SDK — the prefix is already kept intact. Code that assembles the <code>messages</code> array itself is what breaks, especially an agent framework that folds history behind a summary on the client. Anyone shipping such a tool for others to run should be more careful still: a developer's account is usually an older one with the check off, so users on new accounts hit it first.
</p>

<h3>How to check</h3>
<p>
    Rather than guessing whether your code edits history, measure it. Send the <code>thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01</code> beta header with <code>prefix_mismatch_behavior</code> set to <code>"drop_block"</code>: that turns the check on even for an account where it is off, and responses come back carrying an <code>input_transformations</code> array. Run one multi-turn session and log it. Empty on every turn means the history is intact. A <code>prefix_binding_mismatch</code> entry means something before that block changed. A <code>model_binding_mismatch</code> entry after a model switch is expected behavior, not a bug.
</p>
<p>
    What to run in production depends on the design. If a changed history can only mean a bug, leave the default <code>"error"</code> and let it fail. If you compact history on purpose, set <code>"drop_block"</code> so only the affected blocks fall away. Either way, watch the 400s or the <code>input_transformations</code> entries.
</p>
<p>
    A few other things are worth picking up while you are in there. The same release added betas for changing effort mid-conversation, for system messages scoped to a single turn, and for receiving inter-tool progress updates as text via <code>display: "updates"</code>. Default behavior shifted slightly too: where Fable 5 batched several tool calls, Fable 5.1 more often issues one per turn. Answer quality holds, but the extra round trips cost tokens and time.
</p>

<h3>22 September — the exception became the default</h3>
<p>
    Three weeks after this was written, <strong>Opus 5.5</strong> arrived (22 September). The specs match Fable 5.1 at 1M context and 128k output, and adaptive thinking is always on there too. The pricing is what differs. Fable 5.1 left the base rate alone and cut a single cache line; <strong>Opus 5.5 cut the base rate itself.</strong>
</p>
