        <tr><td>Cache reads</td><td>$0.50 (10% of input)</td><td>$0.20 (<strong>5%</strong>)</td><td>$0.25 (2.5%)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Default effort</td><td><code>high</code></td><td><strong><code>medium</code></strong></td><td><code>high</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>
    The price table alone says 20%. The figure Anthropic gave on 24 September in its <a href="https://claude.com/blog/claude-opus-5-5-built-for-coding-sessions-that-use-more-context" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blog</a> is larger: for typical workloads billed by token, Opus 5.5 costs <strong>about 40% less to run</strong> than Opus 5. It rests on three things. Input and output prices fell 20%, <strong>the cached-read price fell 60%</strong> ($0.50 to $0.20), and the model finishes the same work in fewer turns. The same post adds a caveat: on a well-scoped task both models take about the same number of turns, and the price cut is all you get.
</p>
<p>
    The Claude Code usage data in that post, covering March to September 2026, shows why one cache line matters so much. Over those six months Claude worked 3.3 times longer on each prompt, made more than 40% more model calls per prompt, and was interrupted 68% less often. Context per request grew 2.6 times, and <strong>the input-to-output token ratio moved from 189:1 to 324:1</strong>. Each output token now comes with more than 300 input tokens read. As shown above, a long session rereads its front section every turn, so most of that input is billed as cache reads. The cache-read price has become the body of the bill.
</p>
<p>
    Four things now return an error, and three of them are the ones above: thinking cannot be disabled, tool calls cannot be forced, and thinking blocks are bound to the model and the history. Only the fourth is new. On the Claude API and Google Cloud the older <strong>computer use tool</strong> (<code>computer_20251124</code>) returns a 400, so integrations move to the <code>computer_toolset_20260801</code> toolset. On Amazon Bedrock the older tool keeps working, so the answer differs by platform.
</p>
<p>
    Two changes that fail nothing may be the more dangerous pair. The first is that <strong>the default effort dropped from <code>high</code> to <code>medium</code></strong>. Swap the model ID without setting <code>effort</code> and you get a model that thinks less than before, with no error and no warning. Carrying the old value over is not right either, since at the same effort setting this model tends to think more per turn; the official guidance is to measure again rather than reuse the setting.
</p>
<p>
    The second is that <strong>the short notes written between tool calls now arrive as thinking blocks rather than <code>text</code></strong>. At the default setting those blocks come back with empty text, so an interface that streamed them to users as progress goes quiet between tool calls. Nothing errors, which makes it easy to miss, and the text returns only once <code>display</code> is set.
</p>
<p>
    The direction of thinking blocks now needs a map. Opus 5.5 reads blocks from Opus 5 and earlier Opus, Sonnet and Haiku models, but <strong>not from Fable or Mythos models.</strong> Going the other way, Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1 do read Opus 5.5 blocks (on the Claude API). So reasoning survives on the way <strong>up</strong> — Opus 5 to Opus 5.5 to Fable 5.1 — and is dropped on other moves. The history check applies identically, on by default for accounts created on or after 31 August 2026.
</p>
<p>
    What makes this matter is not that the rules changed but that <strong>the place they sit changed.</strong> In early September these conditions were attached to an expensive specialty model and to one behind a door, which made them a question of whether to migrate. Claude Code 2.1.280, released the same day, made Opus 5.5 the <strong>default Opus model</strong> and switched the default on Pro and Team Standard plans from Sonnet to Opus. The model you get without choosing now carries these conditions.
</p>

<h3>October: the 5.5 generation is complete, and the door has three panels</h3>
<p>
    In the two weeks after Opus 5.5, the rest of the 5.5 generation shipped: <strong>Sonnet 5.5</strong> (28 September) and <strong>Haiku 5.5</strong> (7 October). Put the price changes side by side and the two go in different directions.
</p>
