<p>
    Sonnet 5.5 took Fable 5.1's route: the base rate stayed put and only cache reads came down. At launch the cache-read price matched Sonnet 5 at $0.20; on 7 October it was halved, which makes it 5% of the input price, the same multiple as Opus 5.5. Anthropic says the cut lowers the cost of most agentic tasks by about 20%. The reason is the 324:1 input-to-output ratio above. Cache reads are the bulk of the bill, so halving that one line moves the whole thing.
</p>
<p>
    Haiku 5.5 went further down Opus 5.5's route. Its base rate is a tenth of Haiku 4.5's, with two caveats. One is the tokenizer: the same text counts as about 30% more tokens than on Haiku 4.5, so the average saving Anthropic reports is about 75%, not 90%. The other matters more. This model is <strong>priced by prompt length.</strong> If a request's input, cache reads included, goes over 100,000 tokens, the whole request is billed at rates five times higher. A long agent session that rereads its whole front section every turn crosses that line quickly. The one-tenth price comes from short classification, routing, and extraction work, which is exactly what Anthropic says this model is for.
</p>
<p>
    The calling rules seen above have moved down the tiers. Sonnet 5.5 also refuses forced tool calls, ties its thinking blocks to the model and the history, and rejects the old computer-use tool on the Claude API and Google Cloud. The value that turns thinking off is now <code>between_tools</code> rather than <code>disabled</code>. As the name says, it only turns off the thinking before the answer, so the short progress notes between tool calls still come back as thinking blocks. Haiku 5.5 picks up the rule that editing history invalidates thinking blocks and the computer-use toolset, and it returns errors for manual thinking budgets, sampling values such as <code>temperature</code>, and prefill (sending the start of the answer in advance). The thinking-block map gained a line too. On the way <strong>up</strong> from Sonnet 5 to Sonnet 5.5 to Opus 5.5, reasoning carries over (on the Claude API and Google Cloud); any other move away from Sonnet 5.5 cuts it.
</p>
<p>
    There is also a new rule. Both models' thinking blocks work <strong>only in the account that produced them, or in an account linked to it</strong>. If another account sends one, the API drops the block and lets the request through. In September a block carried which model made it and on what history; now it also carries whose account it was made in. For a tool that replays stored conversations through a different account, such as an evaluation harness or a service that moves conversations between teams, that is one more place where reasoning gets cut.
</p>
<p>
    The numbers have converged across the industry as well. Sonnet 5.5's input and output rates ($2 and $10) match OpenAI's GPT-6.1 Sol, released the next day, and the introductory price of Google's Gemini 4 Argon, released two days later, and all three charge $0.10 for cache reads. Haiku 5.5's base rate ($0.10 and $0.50) matches OpenAI's GPT-6 Luna. Once the price lists match, the deciding factors move off the list: thresholds like 100,000 tokens, what happens after an introductory period (Google says Argon goes to $4 and $20), and the calling rules this article has been about.
</p>
<p>
    The door changed as well. On 6 October Anthropic merged Project Glasswing and the Cyber Verification Program (CVP, a program that lowers cyber blocks for verified security organizations) into a single <strong>door with three panels</strong>. Generally available models keep conservative safeguards that block most cyber work, and verified organizations get those blocks lowered in steps. All three tiers use Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Mythos 5.1, and future models.
</p>
