<p>
    Even in the Red Team tier, actions that could hurt people or cause mass disruption, such as deploying ransomware or damaging physical systems, are blocked in real time. Data retention is required in principle so that misuse can be monitored. Anthropic's test results show the door really does open differently at each panel. It ran Opus 5.5 through CyScenarioBench, an evaluation of planning and carrying out multi-stage cyber operations, 10 challenges five times each. Without CVP, all 50 attempts were blocked on the first prompt. In the Defense tier, 46 were blocked partway through. In the Red Team tier, nothing was blocked and 34 succeeded, effectively the same as the 67.6% success rate with no safeguards at all.
</p>
<p>
    In September this article said the same capability went out under two names with a door on only one of them. Now Mythos 5.1 is used not only by Glasswing participants but also by organizations in the Defense tier. What was one closed door and one open door has become <strong>a door split into three panels by identity and purpose</strong>. With Opus 5.5 this article said the stretch where following the discipline was optional had ended. With the whole 5.5 generation out, that discipline now reaches the cheapest tier, and the door has been divided more finely. The calling rules spread downward while the door on dangerous capabilities is subdivided at the top.
</p>

<h3>Why these belong together</h3>
<p>
    The cheaper-model story and the breaking-change story circulated separately, yet both sentences ask for the same thing — <strong>leave the opening alone and only append to the end.</strong> The conditions that keep the cache warm and the conditions that keep thinking blocks valid are the same conditions. The documentation says as much: describing the append-only patterns, it adds that they also keep the prompt cache warm. The cache discount is the reward for that discipline; the history check is the penalty for breaking it. Migrate for the reward alone and you meet the penalty.
</p>
<p>
    Opus 5.5 pushed both a step further. The reward widened from one cache line to the base rate, and the penalty now reaches people who <strong>took the default</strong> rather than people who picked a specialty model. The stretch where following the discipline was optional has ended.
</p>
<p>
    <a href="#!news-20260820-who-makes-the-call">Who Gets to Make the Call</a> traced a movement that pushes judgment toward the machine. How Mythos 5.1 ships is a case from the other side: given the same capability, <strong>a person verifies the organization before the door opens.</strong> How far automation goes there is set not by capability but by the size of the damage.
</p>
<p>
    The stateless turn covered in <a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">Defaults That Stop Asking</a> comes back here too. Remove the session as a container and the question of where state lives is still open. Stamping each thinking block with which model produced it and over which history is one answer: instead of holding a connection open, <strong>each fragment carries where it came from</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Fact-check note</h3>
<p class="scn-sub">The price table ($10 base input, $50 output, $0.25 cache reads, a 0.025 cache multiplier), the specs (1M context, 128k output, 30-day retention), the three breaking changes and their stated reasons, <code>prefix_mismatch_behavior</code>, <code>input_transformations</code>, the August 31 account condition, and the facts that Mythos 5.1 skips the history check and ships only to Project Glasswing all follow the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/fable-5-1/whats-new-fable-5-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official release documentation</a> and the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/fable-5-1/migration-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">migration guide</a>. Multipliers reported at launch, such as "double the Terminal-Bench-Science score" and "over 30% better agentic coding," <strong>do not appear in the official capability write-up</strong> and are not used here; that evaluation carries a reported standard error of 3.5 to 4.5 points per model, which makes a single comparison a weak basis for a multiplier. The 25% and 45% savings figures are Anthropic's own, and a workload where cache reads are a small share of spend will not fall by that much. The Opus 5.5 material (release date, $4/$20 pricing, $0.20 cache reads at a 5% multiplier, the <code>medium</code> default effort, the 1M and 128k specs, the four breaking changes, the thinking-block direction, and the platform split on the computer use toolset) follows the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/opus-5-5/whats-new-opus-5-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official release documentation</a> and the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/opus-5-5/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">model page</a>; the default-model change was confirmed in the <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/changelog" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code 2.1.280 changelog</a>. <strong>The "about 40% cheaper to run" figure is now included as an official number from Anthropic's <a href="https://claude.com/blog/claude-opus-5-5-built-for-coding-sessions-that-use-more-context" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blog</a> of 24 September</strong> (for token-billed workloads). It derives from the 20% price cut, the 60% cached-read cut and fewer turns, and the same post notes that on well-scoped tasks the saving is only the price cut. When this article was first updated on 23 September the official documentation gave no such multiplier, so the figure was left out and put down to the lower default effort. The official basis is the cache-read cut and turn count, not effort, so that guess is corrected here. The usage figures (3.3 times longer per prompt, more than 40% more model calls, 68% fewer interruptions, 2.6 times the context, input-to-output ratio from 189:1 to 324:1) come from the same post and cover March to September 2026. The Sonnet 5.5 part of <strong>the October section</strong> (release date, breaking changes and <code>between_tools</code>, the direction of thinking blocks, account binding, the $0.10 cache read and its 5% multiple) follows the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/sonnet-5-5/whats-new-sonnet-5-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official release notes</a> and the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pricing documentation</a>. The cache-read price was $0.20 at launch and was cut on 7 October. &ldquo;About 20% lower cost on agentic tasks&rdquo; is Anthropic's own figure. The Haiku 5.5 part (7 October release, two price levels around 100,000 tokens, length counted with cache reads included, 1M context and 128K output, about 30% more tokens from the tokenizer, breaking changes, account binding) follows the <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/haiku-5-5/whats-new-haiku-5-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official release notes</a> and the pricing documentation, and &ldquo;about 75% average saving&rdquo; is from <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-haiku-5-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic's announcement</a>. Competitor prices (GPT-6.1 Sol at $2 and $10 with $0.10 cached input; Gemini 4 Argon at an introductory $2 and $10 with 95% off cached input, later $4 and $20; GPT-6 Luna at $0.10 and $0.50) follow <a href="https://venturebeat.com/technology/openais-gpt-6-1-sol-offers-astra-like-performance-at-1-5th-price-a-new-ultrafast-tier-clocks-at-300-tokens-per-second" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VentureBeat</a>, <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-4-argon/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's official blog</a>, and <a href="https://venturebeat.com/technology/anthropic-launches-claude-haiku-5-5-with-90-api-price-reduction-matching-gpt-6-luna" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VentureBeat</a>; performance comparisons were left out because different benchmarks point different ways. The Cyber Verification Program (the three tiers, who qualifies and how long review takes, real-time blocks in the Red Team tier, data retention, the 50-attempt CyScenarioBench results, Glasswing members moving to the Specialized tier) follows Anthropic's <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/cyber-verification-program" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6 October announcement</a>. The same announcement's &ldquo;at least 129,000 verified vulnerabilities from April to July&rdquo; is a lower-bound estimate drawn from a subset of partners' surveys, so it is not used in the body.</p>
